---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro"
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How to See — Culture Explorer"
  description="A practice for attention: look before you read. Train judgment through sustained looking."
>
  <div style="position:relative;min-height:56vh;display:grid;align-items:end;overflow:hidden;">
    <img
      src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/2676582/pexels-photo-2676582.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=2000"
      alt=""
      style="position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover;"
    />
    <div style="position:absolute;inset:0;background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(20,14,10,0.2),rgba(20,14,10,0.72));"></div>
    <div class="wrap-narrow" style="position:relative;z-index:2;padding:4rem 0 3rem;color:#f7f1e6;">
      <div class="kicker" style="color:#e6c48a;">The method</div>
      <h1 style="color:#fff;">Look before you are told.</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="color:rgba(247,241,230,0.9);">
        Most people spend eight seconds with a painting and a lifetime with ugliness. This is a gym for attention.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <p>
        The site is not here to replace standing in front of the thing. It is here to teach a sequence that museums, schools, and feeds have quietly abandoned.
      </p>
      <ol style="font-size:1.15rem;">
        <li><strong>Look.</strong> Set a timer. Two minutes first, then five, then ten. Do not read the caption.</li>
        <li><strong>Describe.</strong> Write only what is there: light, weight, gesture, damage, silence.</li>
        <li><strong>Ask what it demanded.</strong> What did the maker have to know, suffer, or believe?</li>
        <li><strong>Ask what it formed.</strong> Piety, proportion, civic pride, fear of death, gratitude?</li>
        <li><strong>Compare.</strong> Place it beside something we now accept as good enough.</li>
        <li><strong>Transfer.</strong> Find the equivalent survival or failure within a mile of you.</li>
        <li><strong>Earn it.</strong> Add it to your Inheritance only after you have looked.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="kicker">Practice objects</div>
        <h2>Start with one work. Finish it.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <a class="card" href="/work-statue" style="background:#221c16;color:#f3eadc;">
          <div class="body">
            <div class="meta" style="color:#cbb896;">Sculpture · 2 minutes</div>
            <h3 style="color:#f7f1e6;">Marble that still commands</h3>
            <p style="color:rgba(243,234,220,0.82);margin:0;">A weathered face. Begin here.</p>
          </div>
        </a>
        <a class="card" href="/work-venus" style="background:#221c16;color:#f3eadc;">
          <div class="body">
            <div class="meta" style="color:#cbb896;">Painting · 5 minutes</div>
            <h3 style="color:#f7f1e6;">Venus arriving</h3>
            <p style="color:rgba(243,234,220,0.82);margin:0;">Botticelli, after you have looked.</p>
          </div>
        </a>
        <a class="card" href="/compare" style="background:#221c16;color:#f3eadc;">
          <div class="body">
            <div class="meta" style="color:#cbb896;">Judgment</div>
            <h3 style="color:#f7f1e6;">What endured / what we accept</h3>
            <p style="color:rgba(243,234,220,0.82);margin:0;">Two images. One question.</p>
          </div>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <h2>This is not formal analysis for its own sake.</h2>
      <p>
        Line, color, and composition matter. They are the grammar. The sentence is civilizational: what a people thought was worthy of stone, gold, time, and risk.
      </p>
      <p><a class="btn" href="/atlas">Enter the Atlas</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>